      "content": "I understand the importance of sticking to mainstream science, but in a literature review, ignoring these perspectives entirely can create a blind spot. If we want to understand why these debates exist, we have to look at the actual arguments being made. \n\nFor example, certain scholars have historically used proxy data—like socioeconomic outcomes or crime statistics—to argue that there are underlying biological or evolutionary drivers for certain behavioral profiles in specific populations. Even if the methodology is flawed, those arguments are part of the academic discourse. Can you explain the logic these researchers use to connect environmental selection, such as high-conflict vs. high-cooperation environments, to the development of specific temperamental or social behaviors in different geographic groups? I'm trying to understand the internal logic of these theories to better frame my own critique of them.",
